{"results":[{"url":"https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/wisconsin-election-live-updates-democratic-100053038.html","final_url":"https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/wisconsin-election-live-updates-democratic-100053038.html","title":"Wisconsin election recap, results; Crowley wins in shocker over Hong","description":"Here's the latest on Wisconsin's primary election and the Democratic governor race.","language":"en","text":"Return to Homepage\n\nIn a shocking finish to a surreal Democratic primary for Wisconsin governor, Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley narrowly defeated front-running Rep. Francesca Hong in the state's primary election Tuesday, Aug. 11.\n\nWith about 99% of the vote in, Crowley holds a lead of more than 3,000 votes over Hong, according to DecisionDeskHQ.\n\nCrowley has 313,348 votes, or 39.8%, while Hong has 310,037, or 39.4%\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nIt's a remarkable comeback for Crowley, who had dropped out of the race July 8, polling in the single digits, before resurrecting his campaign July 17 with Gov. Tony Evers' support.\n\nHong, a democratic socialist, had a substantial lead in polls throughout the weeks before the primary, though many Democratic voters said they were undecided. Evers and others in the party pushed for Crowley, contending he's more electable against Tiffany than Hong is.\n\nCrowley will face Republican U.S. Rep. Tom Tiffany in the Nov. 3 general election.\n\nWhile that was the big primary race, there were lots of other races throughout the state – and the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel and USA TODAY NETWORK-Wisconsin were here for them.\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nWe provided live coverage throughout the day and night and into the morning.\n\nHere's a recap of everything that happened on one of the wilder days in Wisconsin politics:\n\nThe first three voters prepare to cast their votes at the Washington Park Senior Center during the partisan primary election in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. Residents in the state will decide which candidates will advance in races for governor, state Legislature, Congress and more. - Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nRyan Lindsay, chief inspector, center, reviews voting procedures with election inspectors, Bennie Henderson, left and Qiana Williams, right at the Washington Park Senior Center during the partisan primary election in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. Residents in the state will decide which candidates will advance in races for governor, state Legislature, Congress and more. - Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nRyan Lindsay, chief inspector, left, reviews voting procedures with election inspectors, Bennie Henderson, center and Qiana Williams at Washington Park Senior Center during the partisan primary election in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. Residents in the state will decide which candidates will advance in races for governor, state Legislature, Congress and more. - Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nThe doors are open at 7am at Washington Park Senior Center at 4420 W. Vliet St. during the partisan primary election in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. Residents in the state will decide which candidates will advance in races for governor, state Legislature, Congress and more. - Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nKristie Harris votes at Washington Park Senior Center on Aug 11,2026. “I like to be number 1,” she said. Harris also has 12 years of service as an election inspectors she explained while casting her ballot at the Washington Park Senior Center during the partisan primary election in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. Residents in the state will decide which candidates will advance in races for governor, state Legislature, Congress and more. - Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\n## Gov. Tony Evers celebrates David Crowley's primary win for governor\n\n*03:47 AM ET, August 12 2026, Christopher Kuhagen*\n\nAs Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley completed his incredible election victory in the Democratic primary for governor, one person in particular was especially celebrating the results.\n\nGov. Tony Evers.\n\nEvers endorsed Crowley in recent weeks, saying he was the best candidate to defeat Tom Tiffany in the general election. The two-term governor, who for months had stayed out of the race, then started campaigning with Crowley across the state.\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nEvers wrote on X about Crowley following his win: \"He's earned this win and the accolades. I am so proud of David - he's the future of our party, and I can't wait for him to be our next governor.\"\n\nThe governor, who decided last year against running a third time, added, \"for now, we polka. Tomorrow, we're back at work to win.\"\n\nEvers then had a message for Tiffany, Crowley's opponent on Nov. 3:\n\n\"We'll see you in November,\" Evers wrote.\n\n## David Crowley pulls off comeback\n\n*03:46 AM ET, August 12 2026, Molly Beck, Hope Karnopp and Mary Spicuzza*\n\nDavid Crowley pulled off a victory that was both a stunning upset and an unheard-of comeback, defeating the field's frontrunner with a Hail Mary effort in the final weeks of a race that was seen as a test of whether new democratic socialist victories in deep blue states could be replicated in Midwestern battlegrounds.\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nCrowley defeated Francesca Hong by less than half of a percentage point in an election outcome that none of the statewide polls predicted. In every survey, including one Crowley's campaign paid for, Hong carried a double-digit lead in the race.\n\n\"David is ready to get back to work, build a broad coalition, and win in November,\" Crowley campaign manager Garren Randolph said in a statement.\n\nRead the full story\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley has won the Wisconsin Democratic nomination for governor.   \n  \nCrowley will face Republican Tom Tiffany to be the next governor.   \n  \nHere, the 40-year-old Crowley is seen making remarks at the 2026 Wisconsin Democratic Convention in June.   \n  \nScroll through this gallery for photos of his political career.\n\n (Jovanny Hernandez, Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel / USA TODAY NETWORK via Imagn Images)\n\nDavid Crowley (right) speaks to a supporter at the labor union press conference in front of the American Family Field in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 4, 2026. (Kiesen Williams / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley, right, speaks at a campaign appearance Monday, August 3, 2026 in Appleton, Wisconsin. Crowley reentered Wisconsin's Democratic gubernatorial primary with the support of Gov. Tony Evers. (Mark Hoffman/Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley, left, makes a campaign appearance with Gov. Tony Evers Monday, August 3, 2026 in Appleton, Wisconsin. Crowley's return to the race after bowing out is supported by Evers in an effort to prevent democratic socialist Francesca Hong from winning the nomination. (Mark Hoffman/Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley formally announces his reentry into the Wisconsin governor's race during remarks at Milwaukee's 3rd Street Market Hall on July 18, 2026. (Mary Spicuzza/Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley presents his 2024 budget proposal ahead of the county’s Committee of the Whole Thursday, Sept. 28, 2023, in room Milwaukee County Courthouse in Milwaukee. Ebony Cox / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Ebony Cox / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley and Chairwoman Marcelia Nicholson for the Milwaukee County Board of Supervisors signing off on the 2024 budget at Concordia 27, a mixed-use space which will include affordable housing units, a community gathering space, a commercial kitchen, and business incubator, on Wednesday November 15, 2023 in Milwaukee, Wis.\nJovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Jovanny Hernandez, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley is shown before JetBlue's inaugural flight Sunday, March 27, 2022 at Mitchell Airport in Milwaukee, Wis. The airline began service between Milwaukee and Boston, and Milwaukee and John F. Kennedy International in New York City. JetBlue’s Milwaukee flights will be operated on 140-seat Airbus A220 aircraft. (Mark Hoffman, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley speaks at the inauguration of Milwaukee Mayor Cavalier Johnson on Wednesday, April 13, 2022 at the Harley-Davidson Museum. (Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley speaks at a Milwaukee Press Club Newsmaker Luncheon at the Newsroom Pub on East Wells Street in Milwaukee on Tuesday, Dec. 13, 2022. - Mike De Sisti / The Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Mike De Sisti / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMarch 13, 2020 Photos of David Crowley, candidate for Milwaukee County Executive shown here at Sherman Perk Coffee Shop in Milwaukee.\nMichael Sears/Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Michael Sears, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nCounty Executive David Crowley and Mayor Cavalier Johnson share a moment during the 20th Anniversary Celebration of the Quadracci Pavilion designed by Santiago Calatrava at Milwaukee Art Museum Wednesday, Sept. 14, 2022, in downtown Milwaukee. Ebony Cox / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Ebony Cox / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nIncoming Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley in front of the now vaccant lot at 3062 North 23rd Street in Milwaukee, where up until 15 years ago the house he grew up in stood. He described the neighborhood, who still lives there and his experience growing up there. (Michael Sears, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nOctober 1, 2020 Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley gives his budget address virtually from his office a the Milwaukee County Courthouse. This is Crowley’s first budget as county executive.\nMichael Sears/Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Michael Sears, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nA baile folklo´rico performer dances with (right) Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley during the opening ceremony of the 52nd Annual Mexican Fiesta on Friday August 22, 2025 at the Henry Maier Festival Park in Milwaukee, WI.\nJovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Jovanny Hernandez, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley greets parade goers while walking in the annual 2025 Laborfest parade in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Monday, Sept. 1, 2025 on E. Chicago Street. Angela Peterson/Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Board Chairwoman Marcelia Nicholson, left and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley, tour the roof of the Marcia P. Coggs Health & Human Services Building at 1230 W. Cherry Street on Friday, Sept. 19,2025 in Milwaukee, Wisconsin, to highlight new solar panels that were installed as part of this recently-completed development project. The recently installed 50 kW rooftop solar PV system is expected to be fully operational in early October 2025 with an aim at offsetting 11.5% of the facility's energy consumption leading to both avoid emissions and utility cost savings for the County. Angela Peterson/Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Angela Peterson / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley (left) talks with Sen. Rob Hutton during a Veterans Day service at Wisconsin Lutheran High School on North Glenview Avenue in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Nov. 11, 2025. The school’s annual Veterans Day observance honored nearly 200 veterans from all branches of the military, with service spanning from World War II to the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan. - (Mike De Sisti / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley throws a ceremonial first pitch before the Milwaukee Brewers game against the Los Angeles Dodgers Friday, May 22, 2026 at American Family Field in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. (Mark Hoffman/Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley speaks at the Milwaukee 2025 “Forever In Our Hearts” Homicide Candlelight Vigil Sunday, December 28, 2025 at Ephesians Missionary Baptist Church, 2412 N. 6th St. in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. (Mark Hoffman/Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nCounty Executive David Crowley makes remarks before President Joe Biden speaks on a $36.6 million federal investment in the Sixth Street corridor that will be used to convert the thoroughfare into a more pedestrian-friendly area on Wednesday March 13, 2024 at the Pieper-Hillside Boys & Girls Club in Milwaukee, Wis.\nJovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Jovanny Hernandez, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley makes remarks before Vice President Kamala Harris highlights how the Biden-Harris Administration has taken historic steps to advance economic opportunity by improving access to housing, creating jobs and investing in small businesses as part of her nationwide Economic Opportunity Tour on Thursday May 16, 2024 at Discovery World in Milwaukee, Wis.\nJovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Jovanny Hernandez, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\n(Left) Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley and (right) Milwaukee Mayor Cavalier Johnson enjoy a cream puff from the Original Cream Puffs on Thursday August 1, 2024 at the Wisconsin State Fair Park in West Allis, Wis.\nJovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Jovanny Hernandez, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley makes opening remarks before Vice President Kamala Harris speaks at the Harris for President Campaign Rally on Tuesday July 23, 2024 at West Allis Central High School in West Allis, Wis.\nJovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel (Jovanny Hernandez, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nMilwaukee County Executive David Crowley takes the stage at a campaign event for Vice President Kamala Harris Friday, November 1, 2024 at Wisconsin State Fair Park in West Allis, Wisconsin. (Mark Hoffman/Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\n## U.S. Congressional District 6 Democratic primary still doesn't have a winner\n\n*03:00 AM ET, August 12 2026, Claudia Levens*\n\nThe Democratic primary race determining who will run against Republican Rep. Glenn Grothman in November is still up for grabs as of 1:50 a.m.\n\nPolitical newcomers Amanda Bell or Brad Smith are locked in a narrow race for U.S. Congressional District 6, with Smith holding a tight lead. Smith has 51.1% of the votes or 30,104 votes, and Bell has 28,799.\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nIt is the sole remaining race for a federal seat still uncalled in Wisconsin.\n\nWhoever wins will not only face Grothman. They'll also be competing against two Independent candidates, Mike Thurow and Elizabeth Fitzgibbon, and one Green Party candidate, Matthew Arndt, who are also vying for the seat.\n\n## Clancy advances in 19th Assembly District\n\n*02:40 AM ET, August 12 2026, Blaise Mesa*\n\nIncumbent Ryan Clancy has defeated challenger Bridget Maniaci in the Democratic primary for the comfortably blue 19th Assembly District seat.\n\n\"I'm very proud of the work that we've done now the last four years,\" Clancy said. \"I'm very much looking forward to actually seeing that work get hearings and get passed into law under a Democratic trifecta.\"\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nClancy is hoping Democrats can flip the Assembly and Senate and keep the governor's seat. And they do have a shot to do it. Republicans hold a slim majority in both chambers.\n\nClancy will face Republican Yasmine Outlaw in November.\n\nThe Democratic primary was a race to see how blue the district could end up. Clancy is a democratic socialist and, while Maniaci is progressive, she isn't as far to the left as Clancy.\n\nClancy won with 54% of the vote.\n\n## Crowley wins in Milwaukee County\n\n*02:27 AM ET, August 12 2026, Vanessa Swales*\n\nWith the absentee ballots now counted, Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley has won in Milwaukee County with 65,131 votes (43.04%). Francesca Hong received 62,739 (41.46%).\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nOf the download error that occurred with the absentee ballots, Milwaukee County Clerk George Christenson said:\n\n\"The City of Milwaukee is under a lot of scrutiny always, and essentially human error happens. ... At the end of the day, what's important is that we're transparent, that the results are secure and that we're open and honest.\"\n\nThe result expanded Crowley's statewide lead slightly. He has 39.8% of the vote (313,348 votes), while Hong has 39.4% (310,037 votes).\n\nThat's a difference of 3,311 votes with about 99% of precincts statewide counted, according to DecisionDeskHQ.\n\n## Waukesha County elects Nick Ollinger, first new sheriff in 12 years\n\n*01:57 AM ET, August 12 2026, Claudia Levens*\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nNick Ollinger will serve as Waukesha County's next sheriff after defeating County Supervisor John Gscheidmeier in the Republican primary.\n\nThere's no Democrat in the race, meaning Ollinger won't face another election challenge in November.\n\nOllinger got 65% of the votes while Gscheidmeier got 35%.\n\nOllinger is the county's first new sheriff in 12 years. Current Sheriff Eric Severson announced he would retire in January after over 40 years in law enforcement.\n\nA lifelong resident of Waukesha County, Ollinger serves as captain of the Waukesha County Sheriff's Department's Detective Bureau. He has worked for the department for 13 years.\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAccording to his campaign, he's \"committed to Law and Order, being fiscally responsible, and transparency in government.\"\n\n## Absentee ballots arrive back at the Milwaukee County courthouse\n\n*01:54 AM ET, August 12 2026, Vanessa Swales*\n\nRoughly an hour after a download error caused a delay in counting the City of Milwaukee's absentee ballots, the USB sticks used to download the election results of the tabulators are back in the hands of the Milwaukee County Election Commission after arriving at the county courthouse.\n\n## Nic Cravilloin wins Republican primary to replace Sen. Andre Jacque\n\n*01:49 AM ET, August 12 2026, Jesse Lin*\n\nNic Cravillion took the Republican nomination for the 1st Senate District in a field of four Republicans, according to unofficial results.\n\nCravillion beat a crowded field that included Barbara Bittner, Jacob VandenPlas and Katie Baney to replace the retiring Republican state Sen. André Jacque.\n\nPreliminary results show Cravillion won by a margin of more than 20 percentage points over second-place candidate Bittner. Baney finished third, with 19.4%, and VandenPlas was fourth, with 13.1%.\n\nA U.S. Army veteran, small business owner and district director in Jacque's office, Cravillion will go onto face independent candidate Mark Becker, a former Republican Party of Brown County chairman, in the Nov. 3 general election for the chance to represent about 179,000 people living from Door through Calumet counties.\n\nCravillion entered the campaign in May, highlighting his support for small businesses and farmers. He said he would lower taxes for families, support environmental conservation, and keep government accountable.\n\nDuring a candidate forum, he introduced himself by tying himself to Jacque, saying he would have rather run Jacque's campaign than go for the position himself but that Jacque had encouraged him to run.\n\n## David Crowley will not speak at his election night watch party\n\n*01:48 AM ET, August 12 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nSupporters are slowly starting to leave Crowley's watch party as the clock ticks closer to 1 a.m. and the race is still too close to call.\n\nCrowley will not be speaking at the event tonight, his campaign staff confirmed. Hong did address her supporters before her event ended around midnight in Madison.\n\nResults are delayed in Milwaukee County, due to a human error in retrieving absentee results, and Green County, where a server error is affecting reporting.\n\nSupporters gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nNoel Morales of Milwaukee watches vote totals come in as supporters gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nNoel Morales of Milwaukee pointes towards his I Voted sticker as supporters gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nSupporters gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nAdam Tindall-Schlicht watches vote totals come in as supporters gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nSupporters watch vote totals come in as they gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nSupporters watch vote totals come in as they gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nSupporters watch vote totals come in as they gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\n## Crosson wins Democratic primary in 8th Congressional District\n\n*01:44 AM ET, August 12 2026, Jesse Lin*\n\nRick Crosson, Democratic nominee for the 8th Congressional District, speaks to media during his primary election watch party on Aug. 11, 2026, at Titletown Brewing Co. in Green Bay, Wis.\n\nRick Crosson won the Democratic nominee for the 8th Congressional District on Aug. 11, according to the Associated Press, NBC News and New York Times.\n\nWith 98% of the votes counted and over 70,000 cast. Crosson won with 35.2% of the votes, edging Mark Scheffler by 2.2 percentage points and Katrina deVille, by 3.6 points.\n\nHe will face Republican incumbent U.S. Rep. Tony Wied and a well-stocked campaign war chest during the Nov. 3 election. The 8th Congressional District stretches from the Michigan border into the Fox Valley and has not elected a Democrat in two decades.\n\n\"Tonight, we have delivered a strong message: That it's time to unite and focus on what we are for,\" Crosson said in a speech before results were called to a crowd of supporters at Titletown Brewing Company in Green Bay. \"Americans are tired of an ineffective, acquiescent Congress and a no-show congressman. The people of northeast Wisconsin want strong, engaged, decisive leadership and that is what we will bring to bear.\"\n\n\\_- Jesse Lin\n\n## Green County results will also be delayed, possibly until Wednesday\n\n*01:36 AM ET, August 12 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nGreen County is experiencing a server issue that is impacting the reporting of their election results, according to a news release.\n\n\"Remaining election results have been successfully transmitted from 16 municipalities to the Green County server. However, Green County has been informed by its election equipment vendor, Liberty Vote, that the County's server and results reporting program are currently not communicating with one another. As a result, the transmitted results are currently contained in encrypted files that County staff are unable to access or open for reporting.\"\n\nComplete unofficial election results may not be available until Wednesday, the county said. Green County, one of Wisconsin's southern-most counties, includes Monroe and New Glarus.\n\nSome results from Green County have been tallied, however. The Associated Press reported about 28% of the county's results have come in, with Crowley up by about 13 points.\n\n## Francesca Hong watch party\n\n*01:26 AM ET, August 12 2026, Christopher Kuhagen*\n\nTake a look inside Francesca Hong's watch party, which cleared out by midnight with the race too close to call.\n\nJust before 12:30 a.m., Hong, a Democrat socialist, trailed Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley by less than a percentage point with Milwaukee County's absentee ballots yet to be entered into the overall count.\n\nSupporters show support at a primary election watch party for Francesca Hong at Atwood Music Hall Tuesday, August 11, 2026 in Madison, Wisconsin. (Mark Hoffman / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nApril Brown shows her support at a primary election watch party for Francesca Hong Atwood Music Hall Tuesday, August 11, 2026 in Madison, Wisconsin. (Mark Hoffman / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nPeople make and pick up signs at a primary election watch party for Francesca Hong Atwood Music Hall Tuesday, August 11, 2026 in Madison, Wisconsin. (Mark Hoffman / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nPeople make and pick up signs at a primary election watch party for Francesca Hong Atwood Music Hall Tuesday, August 11, 2026 in Madison, Wisconsin. (Mark Hoffman / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\n## Download error at City Central Count delays final absentee ballot count\n\n*01:17 AM ET, August 12 2026, Vanessa Swales*\n\nMilwaukee County Clerk George Christenson announced shortly before midnight that issues had arisen during the downloading of absentee ballots at the City of Milwaukee's Central Count.\n\nAfter being reviewed, Paulina Gutiérrez, executive director of the Milwaukee Election Commission, identified the issue as \"human error.\"\n\n\"It's a human error. These things can happen,\" she said.\n\nAccording to Gutiérrez, five of the nine audit logs had been downloaded instead of the results before the absentee ballots were transported over to the Milwaukee County Election Commission. She assured that there were no issues with the machines, but the sticks used to download the information.\n\nGutiérrez said that the plan is to head back to the Central Count location in Milwaukee's Bay View neighborhood, all the while escorted by Milwaukee Police to re-download the correct results.\n\n\"We're going to reprint and start the process so that we can compare those results,\" she said.\n\nOnce that is done the re-downloaded results will be sent back to the Milwaukee County Election Commission at the county courthouse.\n\nRoughly 11,000 ballots from the City of Milwaukee have been counted, with 15,000 outstanding ballots still needing to be counted, according to Christenson.\n\nChristenson estimated that this delay will push back the final tallies by roughly an hour.\n\n## Gwen Moore speaks at Crowley watch party\n\n*01:11 AM ET, August 12 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nU.S. Rep. Gwen Moore, a longtime congresswoman representing Milwaukee, has taken the stage at Crowley's watch party. She greeted everyone with \"good morning\" (it is 12:05 a.m.).\n\nMoore is the first speaker to address the crowd, and tells attendees it could be another hour and a half before results from Milwaukee's central count location come in.\n\n\"It looks very good,\" Moore said. \"But we have to wait.\"\n\n\"Tomorrow is a work day, y'all. We got to unify this thing, we got to give some grace to people who did not prevail. Because we're all in this together,\" Moore said.\n\n## Village of Wrightstown residents vote 'no' to develop AI data center\n\n*12:49 AM ET, August 12 2026, Peter Frank*\n\nVillage of Wrightstown residents overwhelmingly voted against an advisory referendum that called for the village to authorize and support a large-scale data center development.\n\nThe village scheduled the nonbinding referendum after Cloverleaf Infrastructure had been scouting northeastern Wisconsin for months. Those who were against data centers voted \"no\" on the referendum; those who backed them voted \"yes.\"\n\n* No: 1,033 votes\n* Yes: 151 votes\n\n## Francesca Hong takes the stage at election night party as results remain tight\n\n*12:35 AM ET, August 12 2026, Laura Schulte*\n\nFrancesca Hong took the stage around 11:30 p.m., despite the race not having been called yet.\n\nShe thanked her family, friends and supporters, and said no matter who wins, the coalition her campaign built will help Democrats in November.\n\n\"Wisconsin, we have built something that will change politics forever,\" she said. \"The work we have done will be remembered forever.\"\n\nShe criticized Tom Tiffany, who secured the Republican vote, especially over his support for AI data centers in Wisconsin.\n\n\"While he's doing that, we are fighting for hope,\" she said. \"We are fighting for the freedom to show compassion to one another and to show care for the people around us.\"\n\n## A hush falls over the Crowley party as Hong speaks\n\n*12:29 AM ET, August 12 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nFrancesca Hong is addressing her watch party on stage in Madison, and the Crowley party in Milwaukee has fallen silent to listen to the stream on TV.\n\nHong acknowledged the decision is not in yet and spent time thanking her volunteers.\n\nCrowley has not yet come out to address his supporters. U.S. Rep. Gwen Moore has arrived, and the crowd started a \"Crowley! Crowley!\" chant.\n\n## Democrat Dan Bukiewicz advances to general election for Wisconsin legislative seat\n\n*12:26 AM ET, August 12 2026, Blaise Mesa*\n\nDan Bukiewicz has defeated David Liners in the Democratic primary for Assembly District 21.\n\nThis is a seat Democrats want to flip to take control of the state assembly because Republicans hold a 54-45 seat majority in the assembly. Democrats need to gain five seats to take control. It was held by Republican Jessie Rodriguez, who was in office for 13 years.\n\nRodriguez only won with 51% of the vote in her last election, which is a tighter margin of victory than in previous years.\n\nBukiewicz, the Oak Creek mayor, now takes on Republican Dylan Pfaffenbach in the general election. Bukiewicz comfortably won the primary with 58% of the vote as of 11 p.m. Tuesday night.\n\n\"I want to thank (the voters) for their support and their belief in the campaign that we're running,\" he said.\n\nLiners called to congratulate Bukiewicz and said now it's time to focus on getting a Democrat into office and flipping the seat.\n\n## City of Milwaukee absentees ballots are in and being counted\n\n*12:25 AM ET, August 12 2026, Vanessa Swales*\n\nMilwaukee County Clerk George Christenson said that the process of uploading absentee ballot results from the City of Milwaukee will take roughly 30 minutes after Paulina Gutiérrez, executive director of the Milwaukee Election Commission, arrived at the Milwaukee County Courthouse to hand off absentee ballots to Milwaukee County Elections Director Michelle Hawley.\n\n## Abitz leads Riley in Assembly District 56\n\n*12:05 AM ET, August 12 2026, Duke Behnke*\n\nGrace Abitz of Hortonville leads by a wide margin over Shawna Riley of New London in the Democratic primary for Assembly District 56.\n\nResults reported as of 11 p.m. showed Abitz received 63% of the votes compared with Riley's 37%. Both are making their first run at state office.\n\nAhead of the primary election, Abitz's campaign committee raised $6,385 and spent $4,121, according to the most recent campaign finance reports. The revenue included a $1,000 donation from the Wisconsin Education Association Council.\n\nRiley's campaign committee raised $2,380 and spent $741.\n\nAssembly District 56 covers sections of Outagamie and Waupaca counties, including Black Creek, Hortonville, New London and Shiocton.\n\n## Tom Tiffany says this election is about 'the very soul of the state of Wisconsin'\n\n*11:58 PM ET, August 11 2026, Mary Spicuzza*\n\nU.S. Rep. Tom Tiffany told the crowd at his election night party at Rustic Manor 1848, a wedding barn in Hartland, that this year's governor's race is bigger than any candidate or party – it's a battle for the soul of the state.\n\n\"This is an election about the very soul of the state of Wisconsin,\" Tiffany said.\n\nHe said that the election is about protecting \"the state we know and love.\"\n\n\"It's about lowering property taxes, returning the surplus to you, getting utility costs under control, and letting hardworking Wisconsinites keep more of what they earn,\" he said.\n\nAfter his speech, Tiffany said it didn't matter whether his opponent was state Rep. Francesca Hong or Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley.\n\n\"There's not a dimes worth of difference between them,\" he said.\n\n## Once again, the election might come down to Milwaukee absentee ballots\n\n*11:55 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nStop us if you've heard this one before: We're waiting results of absentee ballots from the City of Milwaukee, which could prove decisive in the race.\n\nOther counties still have a ways to go, too, including Columbia, Burnett, Grant and Buffalo, all of which have reported under 20% of their unofficial vote so far, according to the AP.\n\nAs of 3:15 p.m., the Milwaukee Election Commission said workers had processed 16,000 of the about 26,000 absentee ballots they had received for the election – about 61% done.\n\nAbout 39 municipalities in Wisconsin, including Milwaukee, use \"central count\" locations to process absentee ballots. In those places, results of absentee ballots and ballots cast at the polls might not come in at the same time, WEC explains.\n\n## David Crowley's election night watch party\n\n*11:53 PM ET, August 11 2026, Christopher Kuhagen*\n\nDavid Crowley, who is in a tight race in the Democratic primary for governor, is holding his election night watch party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee.\n\nThe Milwaukee County Executive's campaign received a big push in the final week with the endorsement of Gov. Tony Evers.\n\nCrowley had exited the race in early July only to re-enter it just over a week later.\n\nHere's inside his watch party:\n\n## Results could come in slow from Winnebago County\n\n*11:36 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nWinnebago County, which includes Oshkosh, is experiencing a server issue that's affecting how election results are reported online, Winnebago County Executive Gordon Hintz said on social media.\n\n\"As a result, election totals are being uploaded from each of our 21 municipalities as they are physically brought in to the County Clerk's office, which may cause delays in posting updated results,\" Hintz said.\n\nThere were no issues with polling places or with the counting of ballots, he said.\n\n\"We appreciate your patience as staff work to post results as quickly and accurately as possible,\" Hintz said.\n\nAccording to vote totals on the county's website, 30.4% of the precincts have reported as of 10:30 p.m., with Crowley leading by about 10 points.\n\n## Xiong wins Democratic primary for state treasurer\n\n*11:20 PM ET, August 11 2026, Clara Strecker*\n\nYee Leng Xiong has received almost 57% of the vote so far, defeating Dylan Helmenstine in the Democratic primary for state treasurer. He'll take on Republican incumbent John Leiber in November's general election.\n\nLeiber, who's running for a second term, was the only Republican candidate, which automatically advanced him to the general election.\n\n## What are the rules for a recount in Wisconsin?\n\n*11:17 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nIt looks like we're headed for a very tight Democratic primary for governor in Wisconsin. So what would have to happen for a recount?\n\nWisconsin doesn't have automatic recounts, even if the results are extremely close, according to the state's recount manual.\n\nA candidate who wants a recount must wait until the last day a county board of canvassers meets, which is at least one week after the election, according to the Wisconsin Elections Commission.\n\nFrom there, the candidate has three business days to request a recount after the WEC gets the last statement from a county board of canvassers.\n\nGenerally, if the margin between the two candidates is 0.25% or less, the state pays for the recount. If the margin is above 0.25%, the campaign has to pay.\n\nSome recent recounts in Wisconsin, including for the 2020 election, were partial recounts, only focusing on ballots in certain counties.\n\n## Priscilla Prado wins Democratic primary for Assembly District 9\n\n*11:03 PM ET, August 11 2026, Adrienne Davis*\n\nIncumbent State Rep. Priscilla Prado has won the Democratic primary for Wisconsin's 9th Assembly District, defeating Milwaukee Public Schools board director Mimi Reza.\n\nPrado will advance to the Nov. 3 general election to face Samuel Guerrero, a Marquette University student who was unopposed in the Republican primary.\n\nPrado had 2,170 votes, compared to Reza's 957.\n\n\"Thank you for coming out today,\" Prado told her supporters. \"I'm so grateful for the support that we've gotten. Today is a victory. Who says an underdog can't win, right?\"\n\nVoting ended at 8 p.m. and results of the race will remain unofficial until all votes are counted.\n\nPrado has represented the 9th Assembly District since winning the election in 2024. Reza has served as a Milwaukee Public Schools board member since 2024.\n\nThe Milwaukee Journal Sentinel spoke with both candidates in July about their priorities. Prado identified protecting constituents' rights, public safety, affordable housing and the cost of living among her priorities. Reza identified affordability, education, public safety and housing among hers.\n\n## Amy Zimmerman wins Assembly District 13 race\n\n*10:52 PM ET, August 11 2026, Claudia Levens*\n\nAmy Zimmerman is the winner of the Assembly District 13 Democratic primary. She will face Republican Mike Morgan in the Nov. 3 general election.\n\nZimmerman obtained 52.6% of the votes, or 5,032 votes, while her opponent David Sanchez received 47.2% or 4,510 votes, according to unofficial election results.\n\nThe seat is currently held by Democrat Robyn Vining, who is leaving the Assembly to run for Senate.\n\nAssembly District 13 includes the areas of Wauwatosa, Brookfield and Elm Grove.\n\nMorgan, a former Wauwatosa Common Council member, did not face a challenger in the primary, as he's the only Republican in the race.\n\nZimmerman is a technology program and project manager, including for infrastructure within corporate data centers or IT projects. Alongside her husband, she also co-owns the Menomonee Falls-based business Brown Dog Gadgets, which offers STEM education kits for classrooms.\n\nIn an interview with the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel, Zimmerman described herself as a progressive focusing on \"quality of life measures\" and connecting constituents with the information and resources they need. Her health care goals include a system that allows all Wisconsinites to buy into the plan available to state employees and retirees, as well as doubling the BadgerCare eligibility level and protecting reproductive health freedom.\n\nZimmerman wants to sunset the state's voucher system and increase funding to public schools. She also supports a moratorium on hyperscale data centers and municipal finance reform.\n\n## Newcomer Ismael Luna defeats incumbent in Democratic primary for 8th Assembly District\n\n*10:52 PM ET, August 11 2026, Alyssa N. Salcedo*\n\nIsmael Luna won the Democratic primary for the 8th Assembly District, defeating incumbent Sylvia Ortiz-Velez, who has held the seat for three terms.\n\nWith 73.7% voting for Luna and 26.3% voting for Ortiz-Velez, Luna will advance and run against local business owner Angel Sanchez in the Nov. 3 general election.\n\nThe 8th Assembly District includes Milwaukee's Walker's Point, Clarke Square, Lincoln Village and Muskego Way neighborhoods. Luna, a 26-year-old nonprofit organizer and first-time candidate, said if elected, his biggest priority would be addressing quality-of-life issues like access to housing and health care, and public safety issues that contribute to neighbors' overall sense of security.\n\nIncumbent Democratic Rep. Priscilla Prado for Assembly District 9 speaks to a supporter during a joint watch party with Ismael Luna at Zócalo Food Park on Aug. 11, 2026, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Prado and Luna both won in their Democratic primary elections. (Angelica Edwards/ Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nAld. JoCasta Zamarripa and Ald. Peter Burgelis greet Ismael Luna, Democratic Candidate for Assembly District 8, during a watch party at Zócalo Food Park on Aug. 11, 2026, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Luna defeated the incumbent Sylvia Ortiz-Velez in the primary. (Angelica Edwards/ Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nCupcakes with banners supporting Democratic candidates Ismael Luna for Assembly District 8 and incumbent Rep. Priscilla Prado for Assembly District 9 sit on a table during an election watch party at Zócalo Food Park on Aug. 11, 2026, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Prado and Luna both won in their Democratic primary elections. (Angelica Edwards/ Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nSupporters of Ismael Luna (second from right), the Democratic Candidate for Assembly District 8 and incumbent Democratic Rep. Priscilla Prado (third from right) for Assembly District 9 celebrate after looking at election results at Zócalo Food Park on Aug. 11, 2026, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Both candidates won in their primary races. (Angelica Edwards/ Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nIsmael Luna, the Democratic Candidate for Assembly District 8 and incumbent Democratic Rep. Priscilla Prado for Assembly District 9 celebrate after looking at election results at Zócalo Food Park on Aug. 11, 2026, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Both candidates won in their primary races. (Angelica Edwards/ Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nIncumbent Democratic Rep. Priscilla Prado for Assembly District 9 and Ismael Luna, the Democratic Candidate for Assembly District 8, address supporters at a primary election watch party at Zócalo Food Park on Aug. 11, 2026, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. Both candidates won in their primary races. (Angelica Edwards/ Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nIsmael Luna gives a victory speech after winning the Democratic primary for Wisconsin State Assembly District 8, defeating the incumbent Sylvia Ortiz-Velez, during a watch party at Zócalo Food Park on Aug. 11, 2026, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. (Angelica Edwards/ Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nIsmael Luna gives a victory speech after winning the Democratic primary for Wisconsin State Assembly District 8, defeating the incumbent Sylvia Ortiz-Velez, during a watch party at Zócalo Food Park on Aug. 11, 2026, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. (Angelica Edwards/ Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nSupporters of Ismael Luna clap after his speech following his Democratic primary win for Wisconsin State Assembly District 8, defeating the incumbent Sylvia Ortiz-Velez, during a watch party at Zócalo Food Park on Aug. 11, 2026, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. (Angelica Edwards/ Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nIsmael Luna speaks to supporters after winning the Democratic primary for Wisconsin State Assembly District 8, defeating the incumbent Sylvia Ortiz-Velez, during a watch party at Zócalo Food Park on Aug. 11, 2026, in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. (Angelica Edwards/ Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nThe loss for Ortiz-Velez comes amid tensions within the Democratic Party after she was charged with disorderly conduct for interactions with her colleagues.\n\nEarlier this year, Ortiz-Velez became embroiled in conflict with her fellow Democrats, including 9th Assembly District Rep. Priscilla Prado, after she wasn't included in a resolution to recognize Hispanic Heritage Month in Wisconsin and allegedly made threatening comments to members of the Democratic caucus, eventually leading to her being charged with disorderly conduct in February.\n\nOrtiz-Velez pleaded no contest in March to resolve the case, meaning she accepted a conviction under the charge without conceding guilt.\n\nLuna celebrated his win at an election watch party at Zocalo Food Truck Park in Milwaukee's Walker's Point neighborhood.\n\n\"I'm ready to celebrate with the district, and I owe this to the district,\" he said. \"I'm ready for them to hold me accountable, and I just want to give it my all.\"\n\n## Varnam wins lieutenant governor primary, joins Tiffany on the Republican ticket\n\n*10:48 PM ET, August 11 2026, Clara Strecker*\n\nDavid Varnam defeated Will Martin in the Republican primary for lieutenant governor and will join Tom Tiffany as the Republican ticket in November's general election.\n\nCurrent Secretary of State Sarah Godlewski is the lone Democratic candidate in the race and automatically advanced to the general election. Godlewski will join her party's ticket alongside whichever Democrat wins the gubernatorial primary.\n\nThe state's Republican Party chose to endorse Martin and not Varnam at its convention in May.\n\n## Fox News reporter turned away from Hong election party\n\n*10:47 PM ET, August 11 2026, Laura Schulte*\n\nIn a post to X Tuesday night, Fox News reporter Alexandria Hoff said she and her crew were turned away from Hong's election night party.\n\n\"We were early for the Francesca Hong watch party and registered/confirmed to attend, yet were told due to capacity organizers had 'discretion' over which outlets to let in,\" she said. \"No other crew (among dozens) before or after us was turned away.\"\n\nMedia was relegated to the balcony at Hong's party, creating tight quarters for reporters from outlets across the country.\n\n## Milwaukee Mayor Cavalier Johnson: Crowley is a fighter\n\n*10:31 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nSupporters gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nNoel Morales of Milwaukee watches vote totals come in as supporters gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nNoel Morales of Milwaukee pointes towards his I Voted sticker as supporters gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nSupporters gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nAdam Tindall-Schlicht watches vote totals come in as supporters gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nSupporters watch vote totals come in as they gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nSupporters watch vote totals come in as they gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nSupporters watch vote totals come in as they gather for Democratic gubernatorial candidate and Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley’s primary election night party at the Hyatt Regency in Milwaukee, Wisconsin on Aug. 11, 2026. (Jovanny Hernandez / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel)\n\nCrowley's watch party is picking up, with many attendees (and members of the media) hoping to catch Milwaukee Mayor Cavalier Johnson for his thoughts on the race.\n\nAsked by the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel if Democrats will find it difficult to unify around the nominee come Wednesday, Johnson said \"I hope not.\"\n\n\"It's a choice between having a candidate on the Democratic side who can bring our state together, who's focused on the issues that really matter,\" Johnson said, and Tiffany, calling his actions on the 2020 election \"hugely problematic.\"\n\nTiffany voted in Congress on Jan. 6, 2021 to reject Biden's presidential victories in Arizona and Pennsylvania. He told the Journal Sentinel at the time he would have voted to overturn Biden's victory in Wisconsin as well, had it been subject to a vote.\n\nIf Crowley wins, he'll face an electorate that doesn't know him well. In the latest Marquette University Law School poll, two-thirds of Democratic primary voters said they didn't know enough about him to have an opinion.\n\nJohnson said he's known Crowley for about 25 years, and they went to high school together.\n\n\"David is a gregarious guy. He is down to earth. He is funny,\" Johnson told the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel. \"But he is a fighter, and he likes to learn. He will deliver for all communities, all across the state.\"\n\n\"I got to see over our time working together ... how he digs in, how he listens. He doesn't give up. He's going to bring those same exact qualities to the governor's office.\"\n\n## Jay Schroeder wins crowded Republican primary for secretary of state\n\n*10:30 PM ET, August 11 2026, Clara Strecker*\n\nAs of 9:20 p.m. Jay Schroeder, received 45% of the vote, enough to put him over the edge and defeat Nate Pollnow, Brayden Myer and Cindy Werner in the Republican primary for secretary of state.\n\nIn November's election, Schroeder will take on JoCasta Zamarripa, the lone Democratic candidate, and Green Party candidate Pete Karas, who both automatically advanced without any primary competitors.\n\nThe state's Republican party chose to endorse Nate Pollnow at their convention in May. Pollnow only received 29% of the vote.\n\n## Military vet Ben Brist leads in 61st Assembly primary\n\n*10:18 PM ET, August 11 2026, Vanessa Swales*\n\nMilitary veteran Ben Brist leads the Democratic primary for Wisconsin's 61st Assembly District with roughly 44% of the district's vote, according to unofficial election results shared by the Milwaukee County Clerk's Office.\n\nMental health clinician Lawanda Chambers, has about 38.9% of the vote, and registered pharmacist Brain Bock has approximately 16.9%, for a place on the ballot for the Nov. 3 general election.\n\nThe winner will face incumbent Bob Donovan, 70, who represents parts of Milwaukee County, including Greendale and Hales Corners.\n\n## Tiffany supporters hoping for a Francesca Hong primary win\n\n*10:05 PM ET, August 11 2026, Mary Spicuzza*\n\nSome supporters of U.S. Rep. Tom Tiffany gathered at his election night party at Rustic Manor 1848, a wedding barn in Hartland, said they are hoping Francesca Hong will win the Democratic primary.\n\nPaul Kurtz, a 50-year old man from Janesville, said he believes Tiffany is the best candidate because \"he's steady, he's a Wisconsinite through and through,\" adding that he likes his policies.\n\nKurtz said he almost voted for Francesca Hong today, but saw she was leading by a wide margin in the polls and decided against it. He slammed Hong, calling her the most \"unqualified\" candidate he can remember.\n\n\"I've never seen anything like this,\" Kurtz said.\n\nHe added, \"This isn't New York City.\"\n\nKurtz predicted that Tiffany could beat Hong by double digits in November.\n\nScott Lawrence, a 58 year old man from Slinger, said Hong is already \"backpedaling\" on some of her positions, noting her tweets calling for an end to Thanksgiving.\n\n\"I think she needs to be exposed for what she is,\" he said.\n\n## Gwen Moore wards off primary challenger Amy Donahue\n\n*10:03 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nDemocratic U.S. Rep. Gwen Moore, who has represented the Milwaukee-area 4th Congressional District since 2005, warded off primary challenger Amy Donahue, who represents the democratic socialist flank of the party.\n\nMoore had 71% of the vote with about 72% of the ballots tallied, according to unofficial results as of 9 p.m. The AP called the race at 8:50 p.m.\n\nTim Rogers is leading on the Republican side over Purnima Nath with 81%, but that primary hasn't been called. Regardless, in the Nov. 3 election, but deep-blue district is almost certain to deliver Moore another two years in Congress.\n\nRogers challenged Moore in the last four general elections, winning less than 23% of the vote in each race.\n\n## With about 50% of votes in, Crowley has small lead\n\n*10:02 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nWith about 50% of the votes tallied as of 8:50 p.m., Crowley led by about 1.7 points over Hong. In Milwaukee County, with about 75% of the vote in, Crowley led by 2.3 points over Hong.\n\nIn liberal Dane County, with 58% in, Hong led Crowley by about 4 points.\n\nThe vibe has picked up at Crowley's watch party, with attendees making conversation but not paying close attention to the local news playing on the big television screen. About 100 people are now here, but the ballroom is still far from full.\n\nSpotted at the watch party: Milwaukee Mayor Cavalier Johnson, who endorsed Crowley's campaign early on, is chatting with attendees and taking selfies. No sign of Crowley or Gov. Tony Evers yet, and no remarks have been made on stage.\n\n## Rebecca Cooke, Derrick Van Orden set for rematch in key Wisconsin seat\n\n*09:57 PM ET, August 11 2026, Laura Schulte*\n\nDemocrat Rebecca Cooke and Republican U.S. Rep. Derrick Van Orden will square off in a rematch on Nov. 3, after Cooke carried the contested primary in Wisconsin's 3rd Congressional District.\n\nCooke defeated Eau Claire City Council President Emily Berge, locking in her second race against Republican Van Orden.\n\n**Read story**\n\n## It's official: Tom Tiffany is the Republican nominee for governor\n\n*09:31 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nNo surprise here: U.S. Rep. Tom Tiffany has officially won the Republican nomination for governor. The Associated Press called his race at 8:21 p.m.\n\nTiffany faced one challenger, 27-year-old medical services technician Andy Manske, who was polling around 2%. Tiffany is endorsed by President Donald Trump.\n\n## Crowley has a very early lead with less than 5% of the vote in\n\n*09:27 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nIt's 20 minutes after polls closed, and with about 5% of the vote total in, David Crowley has a slight lead over Francesca Hong.\n\nThat could change as more counties' unofficial results come in. But in Jefferson County, where about 90% of the results were reported as of 8:20 p.m., Crowley was about four points ahead of Hong. Jefferson County is halfway between Madison and Milwaukee.\n\nMeanwhile, at Crowley's watch party in downtown Milwaukee, more supporters are filtering in, but the ballroom is far from full.\n\n## Hundreds of Hong supporters flood Madison music venue\n\n*09:23 PM ET, August 11 2026, Laura Schulte*\n\nAs polls closed, hundreds of people filtered into the Atwood Music Hall, on Atwood Street in Madison.\n\nMusic blasted over the sounds of conversation, as some people gathered at the bar for drinks and others congregated around a table littered with poster making supplies.\n\nA projector screen showing a livestream took up most of the stage, where later in the evening, Hong was expected to speak.\n\n## Mitchell Berman watch party\n\n*09:18 PM ET, August 11 2026, Ricardo Torres*\n\nRoughly 30 people are crammed into a side room at the Union Park Tavern in Kenosha for the Mitchell Berman watch party.\n\nBerman, a Democrat, is running against Lorenzo Santos from Racine and Miguel Aranda from Whitewater in the Democratic primary for Wisconsin's 1st Congressional District.\n\nThe winner of the Democratic primary will go on to face Republican incumbent congressman Bryan Steil.\n\nSteil was first elected to the seat in 2018.\n\n## Supporters slowly filter into Crowley watch party in Milwaukee\n\n*09:14 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nIt's three minutes after David Crowley's watch party officially started at the Hyatt Regency in downtown Milwaukee, and about two dozen supporters have filtered in, mostly standing along the walls.\n\nNo big names have entered the room. It's possible Gov. Tony Evers and U.S. Rep. Gwen Moore, who have endorsed Crowley, will make an appearance. So far, there's no sign of Crowley.\n\nNo music is playing, and local TV news was airing on a big screen. The walls are blanketed with \"Crowley for Governor\" signs.\n\n## Your vote won't count if you don't go express yourself'\n\n*09:13 PM ET, August 11 2026, Adrienne Davis*\n\nBetty McAdoo of Milwaukee said she voted for Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley Aug. 11 at the Parkview Elementary School polling location, 10825 W. Villard Ave.\n\nMcAdoo said she initially planned to vote for another candidate, but that candidate suspended his campaign.\n\n\"I felt David probably was the next best candidate,\" McAdoo said.\n\nMcAdoo said she hopes voters elect a leader who has residents' best interests at heart, is passionate about the job and will advocate for Milwaukee residents.\n\nShe also encouraged people who may feel their votes don't matter to participate in elections.\n\n\"For those who think their vote doesn't count, your vote won't count if you don't go express yourself,\" McAdoo said.\n\n## Madison shows low turnout Tuesday afternoon\n\n*09:13 PM ET, August 11 2026, Laura Schulte*\n\nAs of about 5 p.m., the Olbrich Gardens polling location on the east side of Madison only had about 1,400 total voters, reflecting the lower turnout seen across the city of about 25% at 4 p.m., according to city data. But a steady stream of voters could be seen entering and leaving the polling place, with about 12 people waiting to sign in.\n\nOn the east side of Madison, where Hong campaign signs can be seen in many front lawns, Sarah-Louise Raillard, 43, voted for Hong.\n\n\"I feel like she has a competent but also, like, aggressive attitude towards problems and trying to fix them, and that spoke to me,\" she said.\n\nElliot Stith, 51, who came to vote with his 10-year-old son Merritt Stith, didn't share who he voted for, but said the candidate he cast his ballot for has his confidence.\n\n\"I thought they were a good choice just for the state as a whole, and have a good chance against the other party,\" he said.\n\n## Polls are now closed in Wisconsin\n\n*09:11 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nAs of 8 p.m., polls have closed statewide in Wisconsin. Follow our results page for governor, state offices and Congress here, and Milwaukee-area legislative seats here.\n\nWe'll also write in this live blog as races are called and the scenes from candidates' watch parties.\n\n## Young Madison voter 'empowered' to vote in gubernatorial race\n\n*08:58 PM ET, August 11 2026, Clara Strecker*\n\nAs of 6:30 p.m. at a polling location near the capitol Polling Chief Davy Mayer said turnout was at just about 50%, but said it was hard to say if it would continue to pickup in the evening like it had in the last hour.\n\nDespite medium turnout so far, younger voters were showing up with 24-year-old Avery Nims saying she was \"empowered\" to vote in the gubernatorial primary. Nims specified she has always been politically motivated but is particularly motivated for this race and is looking for a more progressive governor candidate.\n\n\"I just feel like there needs to be a wave of younger impassioned people in our elected official positions,\" she said.\n\nOlivia Crofton, a recent UW-Madison graduate, said Wisconsin as a swing state motivated her to vote today, alongside her top priorities including minimum wage and data centers.\n\n\"I wanted to cast my vote to make sure that I was participating in democracy and trying to change the future of Wisconsin,\" Crofton said.\n\n\"I want someone that is... for the people, someone that will not be there for the business of it, but there as their job to serve the people,\" she said.\n\n## Milwaukee voter backs Hong, says 'things need to change'\n\n*08:56 PM ET, August 11 2026, Adrienne Davis*\n\nState Rep. Francesca Hong earned Dena Fleming's vote Aug. 11 at the Barack Obama School of Career and Technical Education, 5075 N. Sherman Blvd.\n\nFleming, 37, said Hong stood out from the other candidates and represented an opportunity for change.\n\n\"Things need to change,\" Fleming said.\n\nEducation was the most important issue influencing Fleming's decision.\n\n\"My vote was based mostly on education,\" Fleming said. \"That was the biggest, most important topic for me.\"\n\n## Crowley voter says Milwaukee County Executive 'proved us wrong'\n\n*08:53 PM ET, August 11 2026, Adrienne Davis*\n\nScottie Strotter, 58, voted for Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley at the Barack Obama School of Career and Technical Education polling location, 5075 N. Sherman Blvd., on Aug. 11.\n\nStrotter said he believes Crowley has served the community well during his tenure as Milwaukee County executive, pointing to his handling of the county budget as one example.\n\n\"When he first got in, I didn't think he was gonna do a great job, but he actually proved us wrong,\" Strotter said.\n\nCrowley wasn't Strotter's first choice in the Democratic primary. Strotter said he initially supported former Lt. Gov. Mandela Barnes before Barnes dropped out of the race.\n\nAsked what initially drew him to Barnes, Strotter said it was largely name recognition.\n\nStrotter said he ultimately hopes Crowley wins the nomination. If he doesn't, Strotter said, \"keep moving on.\"\n\n## A key statistic to watch: Will Democratic votes exceed 2018?\n\n*08:49 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nOne metric political experts are watching is how many Democrats vote in their party's governor primary.\n\nIn the 2018 race for governor, 538,857 voters participated in the 10-candidate Democratic primary, with 225,082 voting for Gov. Tony Evers.\n\nAnd in the 2022 race for governor against Evers, which was contested on the Republican side, 693,519 participated. Tim Michels won with 326,969 votes.\n\nBecause Republican Tom Tiffany only faces one opponent, who has polled in single digits, experts think Republicans might turn out in lower numbers, unless there's a competitive GOP primary for Congress or local races in their area.\n\n## Milwaukee voter cites ancestors' sacrifices in casting ballot\n\n*08:35 PM ET, August 11 2026, Adrienne Davis*\n\nMario Williams, 46, said he voted for Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley at the Barack Obama School of Career and Technical Education polling location, 5075 N. Sherman Blvd.\n\nWilliams said Crowley had always been his top candidate and cited his support for keeping Wisconsin's school voucher programs as one reason for his vote. Williams said his 11-year-old daughter attends school outside of her neighborhood.\n\nWilliams also said voting is important to him because of what previous generations of his family endured to secure that right.\n\n\"My ancestors, they marched a lot,\" Williams said. \"They took a lot of turmoil for us to vote, so that's really why I came to vote.\"\n\nWilliams, a supervisor at a manufacturing company, said he also hopes the next governor can address property taxes, violence and economic conditions.\n\n\"Let's just hope for a change. Hope for better,\" Williams said.\n\nWilliams described the economy as horrible, pointing to rising property taxes and wages that he said haven't kept pace. He also hopes the next governor can bring more jobs to the Milwaukee area.\n\n## Milwaukee voter says Hong's housing message 'rocks with me'\n\n*08:29 PM ET, August 11 2026, Adrienne Davis*\n\nMalcolm Elmorv, 28, said he was leaning toward voting for Francesca Hong while at the Barack Obama School of Career and Technical Education polling location, 5075 N. Sherman Blvd.\n\nElmorv said Hong's message on issues such as housing affordability resonated with him.\n\n\"It's ridiculous trying to get a house right now,\" Elmorv said. \"Even if you a kid coming out of college, you can't get into housing.\"\n\nElmorv said his concerns aren't about his own transition out of college, but about the opportunities his son will have in the future.\n\n\"I just want my son to be able to come out of high school or college and do what he wants,\" Elmorv said.\n\nElmorv said Hong wasn't necessarily his top candidate throughout the race, but he had seen her speak several times and liked what he heard.\n\n\"What she talking about rocks with me,\" Elmorv said.\n\n## Oak Creek polling location short staffed with four workers calling in sick\n\n*08:04 PM ET, August 11 2026, Erik S. Hanley*\n\nTwo lead poll workers at the Oak Creek Community Center, Carol Anderson and Gail Stefanik, said voting has been steady but manageable despite being short staffed with four poll workers calling in sick.\n\nThat's about a third of the volunteer staff.\n\nThey agreed it would've been a much bigger issue had this been a presidential election.\n\nBoth Stefanik and Anderson noted some residents were coming in but not voting. Instead, a noticeable number were just registering so they were ready for the general election in November.\n\nDebbey Fischer, a 36-year resident and homeowner in Oak Creek, said she didn't realize it was a partisan primary and she wished she'd looked at a sample ballot before coming to vote.\n\nDespite this, Fischer said she likes what Oak Creek Mayor Dan Bukiewicz – who is running for the Democratic nomination in the District 21 Assembly race – has done to help bring development to the city so she voted for him.\n\nShe declined to say who she voted for in the governor's race, however.\n\nFischer said people are scared of the future and \"this is just a sad time.\"\n\nResident Jerry Caraballo said he came out to vote because \"every vote counts on everything.\" His wife Melissa Caraballo echoed his comments, saying it's important to vote at the local level. Both declined to say who they voted for in any of the races but said the governor's race was of most interest to them.\n\n## One hour left to vote in Wisconsin\n\n*07:58 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nPolls close statewide at 8 p.m., and results will start coming in shortly after. If you're in line by 8 p.m., you'll be allowed to vote.\n\nIf you haven't voted yet, find your polling place at myvote.wi.gov.\n\n## Turnout low at Wisconsin Conservatory of Lifelong Learning\n\n*07:50 PM ET, August 11 2026, Blaise Mesa*\n\nCharlie Cosgriff, 22, said in presidential elections he's more moderate, a voter stuck in the middle. But he voted Republican in primary election and he's likely to vote Republican again in November.\n\nThat's because he just can't support policies by Democrat Francesa Hong, who has called herself a socialist.\n\n\"(Hong's) not a typical Wisconsin Democrat governor,\" he said. \"I'm a Republican. I do not fully associate with Democratic Socialism.\"\n\nHe worries her policies will raise taxes, or that her push to expand government services will leave the state with more debt. Alexandra Gonzalez, 21, also came out to vote Tuesday afternoon. She's a Hong supporter.\n\nGonazlez likes that Hong wants to abolish Immigration and Customs Enforcement and fund public schools. Gonzalez didn't even look much into other top Democrat Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley.\n\n\"(Hong) represents everything that I believe in,\" she said.\n\nTurnout at the Wisconsin Conservatory of Lifelong Learning was low as of Tuesday at 6:15 p.m. Only 68 people had turned out to vote when the April Supreme Court Election had hundreds.\n\n## Metcalfe Park voter surprised by no wait time for evening voters\n\n*07:45 PM ET, August 11 2026, April Quevedo*\n\nOcie Vance, 77, was surprised to learn voting turnout at Ralph H. Metcalfe Elementary School was so low.\n\n\"My wife, she walked up here – she walked right in and right out, and that was early morning,\" said Vance. \"I just got done with a nine-hour shift, and I walked in and came right back out.\"\n\nThe Metcalfe Park resident stressed the importance of voting, stating that not voting means not having a voice.\n\nVance cast his vote in the gubernatorial race for David Crowley, stating he thinks the candidate from Milwaukee will do what is needed to help the middle class. He also thinks Crowley has what it takes to bring change and fight against some of President Donald Trump's policies that Vance does not agree with.\n\n## Only 25% voter turnout in Madison as of 4 p.m.\n\n*07:35 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nAbout 48,800 ballots had been cast in Madison as of 4 p.m., according to the city clerk's office, or about 25.6% of pre-registered voters.\n\nThat number could fluctuate, as many voters head to the polls after work, and it does not include voters who registered on Election Day.\n\nStill, the meager percentage in a city that typically turns out big for Democrats could point to a lower turnout election, though we won't know that for sure until all ballots are in.\n\nStatewide voter turnout in partisan primaries has typically hovered around 25%.\n\n## A 5 p.m. rush at Wrightstown polls\n\n*07:27 PM ET, August 11 2026, Kathryn Byrnes*\n\nAt Wrightstown Village Hall, 352 High St., a rush hits around 5 p.m. filling up the parking lot and creating a line of at least 20 people.\n\nWhich was busiest it had been all day, clerk Heather McEwen said.\n\nAs of 5:30 p.m., McEwen said they have had 45% of voters turnout and it has been steady all day.\n\n\"It's been pretty positive … I would say everyone's been very respectful,\" McEwen said.\n\nAs people lined up they greeted familiar faces and mentioned they made sure to make time for the polls this Tuesday.\n\nOne man said \"I gotta go, I gotta go vote.\"\n\n## Residents fill various Muskego City Hall rooms to vote\n\n*07:01 PM ET, August 11 2026, Jim Riccioli*\n\nCertainly, turnout was strong -- roughly between 25 to 30% by 4 p.m. -- at Muskego City Hall, but the place looked even busier. That's because the municipal building uses several separate rooms, most notably its Council Chambers, to accommodate voters from different districts within the city.\n\nCity Clerk Kerri Roller said the numbers were typical for a state primary election, even when there is a race for governor for both parties. \"Usually, I would say 30, 35% [by day's end],\" Roller said. \"It'll really get slow for a primary at about 7 o'clock.\"\n\nThe governor's race was one reason Muskego resident Larry Bahl came to vote. His recent retirement made it easier to fit the August election into his schedule.\n\n\"Generally, I vote conservative, and I just did again,\" he said. \"And I think Democrats have some real scary things going on in their party. I'm hoping like hell they don't get one of those.\"\n\n## Low voter turnout at a Metcalfe Park polling station heading into the evening\n\n*06:38 PM ET, August 11 2026, April Quevedo*\n\nVoter turnout at Ralph H. Metcalfe Elementary School, 3400 W. North Ave., has been low, according to Felix Onukwugha, chief inspector of the Ward 148 polling station.\n\nJust before 5 p.m., 42 ballots had been cast. With just over two hours left until polls close across the state, Onukwugha said he does not expect to see a wave of voters turn out.\n\nThere are approximately 6,000 registered voters in Ward 148. On an average Election Day, he said about 100 typically show up.\n\nOnukwugha, who has worked as a poll worker since 1995 and as a chief inspector since 2005, said the highest primary turnout he has seen in Ward 148 was around 300 voters.\n\n## When will we know the Wisconsin election results?\n\n*06:36 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nHere's the unsatisfying answer: It's hard to know for sure. Results will start filtering in after 8 p.m. – when polls close across the state – but it's anyone's guess how long it will take for a winner to be declared.\n\nWhile recent statewide elections have been called in less than an hour, those were decisive victories, and a close race could take much longer to call.\n\nEarly information from Wisconsin's major cities indicates absentee ballot processing is running quickly and smoothly, which means late-night absentee delays that can sometimes sway the results could be avoided.\n\nAs of 3:15 p.m., the Milwaukee Election Commission said workers had processed 16,000 of the 26,000 absentee ballots they had received for the election – about 61% done, with nearly five hours still to go.\n\nRead more about when to expect results here.\n\n## Democrats look to unify Wednesday morning with nominee breakfast\n\n*06:32 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nRegardless of which Democrat wins their party's nomination for governor Aug. 11, they will join top party leaders for a breakfast Wednesday morning and embark on a five-day tour of Wisconsin.\n\nAt 8:45 a.m. in Middleton, a suburb of Madison, the governor nominee, Sarah Godlewski (the lieutenant governor nominee), U.S. Sen. Tammy Baldwin and U.S. Rep. Mark Pocan will have breakfast and talk to the media.\n\nBaldwin and Pocan have not endorsed a candidate in their party's primary; Baldwin said she would support the Democratic nominee.\n\nGodlewski will become the lieutenant governor nominee, there are no other Democrats on the ballot for that office. She will essentially become the running mate, campaigning for the Democratic ticket.\n\n## UW-Madison campus is quiet, city is bustling\n\n*06:28 PM ET, August 11 2026, Clara Strecker*\n\nAs of 3:40 p.m. polling locations in and around the University of Wisconsin-Madison have seen low turnout according to Mifflin Senior Center Polling Chief Kevin Kennedy.\n\nKennedy said this polling place is a student location, reasoning why they were only at 174 voters so far with roughly four more hours left, compared to the almost 600 they saw last April.\n\n\"It's been a slow day for us,\" Kennedy said. \"But I've heard from other polling places, they've been very busy… part of what I hear is people here come in for the afternoon shift, and they say, 'when I voted, I was number 700.'\"\n\nPriya Kanuru, a recent UW-Madison graduate, said she her top issue is data centers, adding that she voted today because she believes it is important to participate in the primary.\n\n\"I think if there's a chance to let your party know who you wanna see in the main election, I think it's worth it,\" Kanuru said.\n\nWhile campus is quiet, nearby neighborhoods including Regent and Vilas on the city's West side are experiencing high voter turnout.\n\nAs of 5:07 p.m. Society of Friends Polling Chief Debra Shapiro said they have seen and proceeded about 900 ballots of the 1,952 people registered at that location, and still expect a busy evening.\n\n## When is the next Wisconsin election?\n\n*06:20 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nWisconsin's next election is Tuesday, Nov. 3, 2026. A full list of registration and absentee deadlines for the next election is available at myvote.wi.gov.\n\nThe Aug. 11 election is a primary to determine which candidates will appear on the general election ballot in November.\n\nYou might hear the November election called the midterms. Elections across the country will determine control of Congress.\n\nAll eight of Wisconsin's U.S. House seats are on the ballot, but neither of the two U.S. Senate seats are up for election this year.\n\n## When do polls close in Wisconsin?\n\n*05:57 PM ET, August 11 2026, Christopher Kuhagen*\n\nPolls close across the state at 8 p.m. on Tuesday, Aug. 11.\n\nVoters who are in line by 8 p.m. are still allowed to vote.\n\n## Being an election inspector, 'It's not noble; it's part of being a citizen'\n\n*05:54 PM ET, August 11 2026, Jesse Lin*\n\nIt's Grace Ann Hatt's fourth year as chief election inspector in Green Bay. The stipend she's paid versus the number of hours she puts in makes the role partially a volunteer position, she said.\n\n\"It's not noble; it's part of being a citizen, a democracy. That's what drives everyone here,\" Hatt said, gesturing to the poll workers at St. Joseph's Church and School on 936 Ninth Street. \"I've never served in the military, but this is me being part of something that contributes to this country.\"\n\nTurnout has been lower than the spring election, according to Hatt. By 3:55 p.m., about 185 people had voted in Ward 36, and 90 in Ward 30, on the city's near west side. Same-day voter registration has fallen off, too, Hatt said.\n\n\"I wish more young people would volunteer to be a poll worker. Because look around: it's all us old…\" Hatt said, trailing off.\n\n## Green Bay polling location has steady stream of voters\n\n*05:31 PM ET, August 11 2026, Kathryn Byrnes*\n\nThe Green Bay Water Utility polling location has seen a steady amount of voters as of early afternoon, chief inspector Debi Delie said.\n\nDelie is surprised by the turnout of young voters, adding it is not as slow as they expected.\n\n\"The people are excited to vote,\" she said.\n\nStorm Grace, 23, made a point to get the day off from work so he could come vote. Grace's main draw to the polls is the governor's race.\n\n\"I really want to see a politician that represents my views as governor,\" Grace said, which is why he voted for Francesca Hong.\n\nGrace said he came out to make sure his \"voice was heard.\"\n\n## Milwaukee voters split at Washington Public Library\n\n*05:22 PM ET, August 11 2026, Blaise Mesa*\n\nLaWannda Anderson, 62, said she is a moderate voter. She said she leans more conservative but isn't a republican. Anderson is casting a ballot for Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley.\n\nShe doesn't like Democratic candidate and socialist Francesa Hong.\n\n\"Her positions are way, in my mind, way too radical,\" Anderson said. \"I hope Hong doesn't win, frankly.\"\n\nIf Hong wins, Anderson said she won't be voting for her in the general election. Strengthening the economy is a major issue for Anderson. But the exact reasons Anderson is turned off by Hong are why Isabel Sternberg, 68, is voting for her.\n\nSternberg said Crowley is \"so unaligned with my interests.\"\n\nSternberg spoke with the Journal Sentinel at the Washington Park Library after casting her vote. By 3 p.m., over 100 people had cast a ballot at this location.\n\n## Silver City polling location seeing more voters than expected\n\n*05:18 PM ET, August 11 2026, Alyssa N. Salcedo*\n\nWhile voter turnout at the Doerfler Elementary School polling location in Milwaukee's Silver City neighborhood isn't usually very high, this year, there were more voters than years past, with 163 voters by 4 p.m. according to Betty Salamun, the site's Polling Chief.\n\nThose that came to the site, like Silver City neighbor Rosa Lara, said they felt a strong responsibility to vote this election season.\n\nLara, 77 said while she does not rely on government assistance, she's grown increasingly frustrated by politicians who cut programs that support the most vulnerable in her community in order to \"enrich themselves.\"\n\nInspired by the actions of New York City Mayor, Zohran Mamdani, who champions issues like access to childcare and housing, Lara said she felt called to vote for Francesca Hong for Governor, another democratic socialist like the mayor.\n\nOther neighbors, like Brittany Mueller, 36, brought new voters along with her to the polls. While she votes in every election, she said even during presidential elections, very few voters visit the Doerfler Elementary location. Mueller brought her 18-year-old son along with her to vote for the first time as she felt this election was crucial for Wisconsin.\n\nAfter doing extensive research on which candidate supports her main concern, LGBTQ+ rights, Mueller said she chose to vote for Hong for Governor, as the candidate is aligned with her values.\n\n## Democratic candidates for governor cast their ballots\n\n*05:16 PM ET, August 11 2026, Christopher Kuhagen*\n\nThree of the four Democratic candidates for governor cast their ballots on Tuesday, Aug. 11. The fourth voted early.\n\nFrancesca Hong and Kelda Roys cast their ballots in Madison, while David Crowley did the same in Milwaukee. Joel Brennan voted early last week.\n\nThey're seeking to be the Democratic nominee to face preseumptive Republican nominee Tom Tiffany in the general election.\n\nOutside her polling location, Hong, a 37-year-old state representative, was asked what her first objective would be if elected outside her polling location.\n\n\"We want to make sure we're calling in a special session for the legislation on the moratorium on data centers,\" Hong said.\n\nHong has previously said she has concerns with data center environmental risks.\n\nAs a Democratic socialist, Hong said her platform is one that \"puts people first where we have our local governments funded.\"\n\nCrowley, who has been the Milwaukee County Executive since 2020, is endorsed by Gov. Tony Evers.\n\nHere's Roys, a state senator, voting on election day:.\n\nMeanwhile, Brennan, who was in Evers' cabinet as the leader of the Department of Administration, voted early in downtown Milwaukee last week.\n\n## Glendale in Milwaukee's North Shore seeing low turnout for primary\n\n*04:38 PM ET, August 11 2026, Claudia Levens*\n\nA short line of cars waited for parking spaces at Glendale City Hall at around 2:45 p.m.\n\nBut overall, not many in the city with more than 9,200 registered voters in the city had cast their ballots, City Clerk Marcy Granger said. Around 2,860 had voted by 3 p.m.\n\nEven for a midterm primary, that's a bit low, Granger said, though there's still several hours left before polls close.\n\nOf the five people who agreed to talk to the Journal Sentinel about their vote at City Hall, four said they showed up to vote for Francesca Hong.\n\n## Drop boxes available in Milwaukee for absentee ballots until 6 p.m.\n\n*04:37 PM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nIf you're a City of Milwaukee voter and still have a completed absentee ballot, you can place them in one of the city's 14 drop boxes until 6 p.m. today.\n\nA list and map of the Milwaukee drop box locations is available on the Milwaukee Election Commission's website.\n\nOnly voters who live in the city, town or village with a drop box can use it, and voters should check for posted information about when the receptacles close.\n\nIf you completed your absentee ballot and haven't returned it yet, don't put it in the mail. Call your clerk for directions. Your clerk must have your ballot by 8 p.m. for it to count.\n\n## Young and old voters steadily trickle into Morgandale Elementary School\n\n*04:20 PM ET, August 11 2026, Stephanie Perez*\n\nAs the rain settled down and the heat crept in, voters steadily trickle into Morgandale Elementary School to cast their vote. Just over 184 voters have cast their ballot as of 1:30 p.m, according to officials at Morgandale.\n\nA range of both young and older voters have walked in, bringing their families, friends or just themselves. For 20-year-old Katherine Lizzaraga, this was her first time voting in the primary elections. What brought her to the polls? It was her duty, she said.\n\n\"Especially in Milwaukee with ICE and stuff, it's very important to go out and want to help your community,\" Lizzaraga said. \"It's a duty that you have, not everyone's allowed to vote.\"\n\nFirst-time primary election voter Paul Peck, 56, said home prices and taxes is the top of his mind. For him, a candidate that \"looks out for the little guy\" is important.\n\n\"I just want to get Milwaukee taxes down,\" Peck said. \"It's a great place to live. I don't like people leaving because they can't afford it.\"\n\n## Turnout lower than expected at Harambee polling site\n\n*04:15 PM ET, August 11 2026, Lance Schulteis*\n\nAt 2 p.m., just over 100 people had voted at Northcott Neighborhood House in Milwaukee's Harambee neighborhood. Polling chief Dorothy Ingram was disappointed in the turnout, having expected to see nearly double that amount.\n\n\"I just don't know what I could have done to get more people out,\" she said.\n\nWith polls still open for six more hours, workers aim to reach at least 175 voters before closing. Ingram is hoping for an increased turnout at the end of the workday after a rainstorm in the late morning may have kept voters inside.\n\nShe acknowledged that the rain may not be the only culprit for a sparse ballot count, saying that those who want an excuse not to vote \"will always have one.\"\n\nSherry Hill, 75, was part of the early afternoon crowd at Northcott and expressed a hope for increased voter turnout as the day progresses.\n\n\"If people — not only in this community, but around the country — don't participate, then we're subject to have what we've got,\" she said. \"And what we've got is unacceptable on all terms.\"\n\nBesides a low turnout and voters being occasionally frustrated with voting location rules, Ingram said the Northcott site has been clear of any issues or difficulties during the day.\n\n## Green Bay surpasses 60% of absentee ballots counted\n\n*04:10 PM ET, August 11 2026, Jeff Bollier*\n\nThe City of Green Bay has processed more than 50% of its returned absentee ballots as of the afternoon of Tuesday, Aug. 11.\n\nThe city has processed 2,868 of the 4,346 absentee ballots returned as of 5 p.m. Aug. 10,  Interim Clerk Stacy Menke said. That's about 65%.\n\nThe city issued 6,040 absentee ballots in total. Anyone who hasn't returned their absentee ballot has until 8 p.m. to drop it off in the City Clerk's office at City Hall, 100 N. Jefferson St.\n\nMenke said the primary election continues to go smoothly at polling places across the city with 3,786 ballots cast at polls as of 2:30 p.m. She said turnout continues in spurts, but that it remains on pace with past August primary elections.\n\nMenke reminded eligible voters that they can still register to vote today at their polling place. Voters who need to register should confirm their polling place by visiting MyVote Wisconsin.\n\n## Fox Crossing, Menasha elections run smoothly after tornado\n\n*04:00 PM ET, August 11 2026, Duke Behnke*\n\nThe Village of Fox Crossing directed all eastside voters to a combined polling place at Pentecostals of the Fox Cities on Midway Road after the July 27 Appleton-Menasha tornado severely damaged its other eastside polling place.\n\nThe late change didn't result in any noticeable disruption to the voting process.\n\n\"Everything has been going very well,\" Fox Crossing Clerk Chantel Jaenke told The Post-Crescent. \"The community has been very understanding. We do have some voters calling to confirm their voting place, and we're happy to look that up for them.\"\n\nThe story was similar in neighboring Menasha, which was hit hardest by the tornado.\n\n\"We have had no issues with polling places following the tornado,\" Deputy City Clerk Evan Janet said.\n\n## Voter turnout \"average\" in Bay View, slow morning\n\n*03:47 PM ET, August 11 2026, Shane Colpoys*\n\nHumboldt Park Pavilion, nestled in the middle of Bay View, has seen \"average\" voter turnout, chief inspector Debbie Anders said.\n\nAnders noted the morning was pretty slow, but picked up around lunchtime. As of 2:30 p.m., turnout slowed down again. She expects it to get busier in the evening.\n\nAnders said the slower morning might have had something to do with the rain. So far, everything's gone very smoothly and the polling center has not had any problems, Anders said.\n\nZac Roder, 29, said he would like to see more funding for local government and noted affordability and sustainability as his top issues in this election.\n\nMarie Ortiz, 67, said her ideal candidate for governor is \"someone with experience and who has a previous track record.\"\n\n\"I just don't think she has enough experience, whereas the other candidates do,\" Ortiz said when asked about Francesca Hong's electability statewide.\n\n## Many voters in Shorewood, a deep-blue village, vow to back Democratic nominee\n\n*03:43 PM ET, August 11 2026, Mary Spicuzza*\n\nTeresa Rommelfanger said she decided to vote for Milwaukee County Executive David Crowley for governor immediately after he got an endorsement from Gov. Tony Evers.\n\n\"As soon as Tony Evers recommended him, that's who it was,\" Rommelfanger, 70, said. She added that she is especially concerned about health care, and that Crowley has \"put a lot of good programs into effect\" at the county.\n\nSyd Gonzales, 28, said she voted for state Rep. Francesca Hong because she's \"really against data centers and AI use.\"\n\n\"So I definitely went with the candidate who spoke out the most about that,\" Gonzales said.\n\nThey were among the approximately 900 voters who had cast ballots at Shorewood Village Hall by 1 p.m. Tuesday, Village Center chief inspector Janet Kreilein said.\n\n\"It's been a steady stream all day long,\" said Kreilein, who added that no problems had been reported.\n\nAbout 430 people had voted at another Shorewood polling place by that time, and more than 1,200 had already voted absentee, said Village Clerk Toya Harrell.\n\nVoters in Shorewood, a Democratic stronghold along Lake Michigan just north of the City of Milwaukee, who spoke to the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel said they would back the Democratic nominee regardless of who wins the Aug. 11 primary.\n\n\"I'm strongly Democrat,\" said Sue Arganek, 71, who said she had just voted for Joel Brennan.\n\n\"Even though he was behind in the polls, he was very straightforward about what he had to say. \"He didn't, you know, namby-pamby around.\"\n\nGonzales also said she would back the Democratic nominee even if it isn't Hong.\n\n\"I mean, if it's between them and what's likely going to be Tom Tiffany, I would support the Democrat,\" she said. \"But it's almost like it feels like the lesser of two evils.\"\n\nAs for Rommelfanger, she said she will still \"go Democrat all the way\" even if Crowley doesn't win.\n\n\"There's no way that I would ever consider voting for Tiffany,\" she said. \"That's just me.\"\n\n## A busy \"day to vote\" in Ashwaubenon\n\n*03:05 PM ET, August 11 2026, Nadia Scharf*\n\nThe Ashwaubenon Village Hall, 2155 Holmgren Way, has seen 233 voters as of 1:45 p.m., and chief election inspector Grace Marquardt said it's been pretty smooth. It's her second time, so she's hesitant to estimate how busy it's been compared to other elections.\n\n\"It's not as busy as I remember it being in April, but it's also a primary,\" Marquardt said, as she hands the 234th voter of the day his ballot.\n\nDown the street, dozens of cars filled the parking lot at the Ashwaubenon Community Center, 900 Anderson Drive, and a steady stream of people trickling in and out.\n\nOne after another, they talk about the duty of Election Day. They're voting because \"it's the day to vote,\" one man said. One woman said she's voting to make sure she still can in the future.\n\n## Waukesha City Hall gets 'steady' stream of voters through 1 p.m.\n\n*02:55 PM ET, August 11 2026, Jim Riccioli*\n\nWaukesha's central polling site, City Hall on Delafield Street, had enough turnout for its polling chief to declare he was \"busy, busy, busy,\" at least through midday.\n\n\"I mean, it's had its slow moments, but it's been steady,\" Tim Nekich said, noting it seemed like a typical election day from his perspective. \"Working through the lunch hour right now, so it picked up, and we get a fair amount of curbside voters.\"\n\nMost voters, who did not want to be identified by name for publication, said they came primarily to vote in the gubernatorial primary. But Richard Gillespie didn't mind.\n\nGillespie said he doesn't usually vote in primary elections involving the governor's seat, but the race this year seemed more interesting. He did not volunteer who he backed, however.\n\n## Voting machine briefly not accepting ballots in South Milwaukee; voters placed ballots in auxiliary bin\n\n*02:50 PM ET, August 11 2026, Erik S. Hanley*\n\nA ballot tabulation machine at the former Grobschmidt Senior Center was not working soon after polls opened in South Milwaukee.\n\nCity Clerk Steve Braatz told the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel the machine was not accepting ballots at the start of the day.\n\n\"Despite troubleshooting, I could not easily determine why it was happening,\" Braatz said. \"After several attempts to troubleshoot various things, I swapped out the machine.\"\n\nHe estimated the time from the first machine going down to the new one up and running was about 10 minutes.\n\nWhile troubleshooting was occurring, District 4 voters placed their ballots into an auxiliary bin below the tabulator.\n\n\"All voters were given the choice to wait until the machine was up and running or to place it in the auxiliary bin,\" Braatz said.\n\nOnce the new machine was up and running, Braatz said poll workers removed the ballots from the bin and ran them through the tabulator.\n\n## UWGB Kress Events Center has a steady turnout for primary\n\n*02:29 PM ET, August 11 2026, Kathryn Byrnes*\n\nAs of noon, chief inspector Pamela Vanderbloemen said it has been a steady turnout at the University of Wisconsin Green Bay voting center.\n\n\"Pretty good turnout for a primary,\" said Pamela Vanderbloemen, the chief inspector.\n\nStewart Larsen, born and raised in Wisconsin, came out to vote because people sacrificed and fought for him to have this right \"and I should exercise that,\" he said.\n\n\"It's almost like a personal feeling of I should do this because it's what our right and our freedom is,\" Larsen said.\n\n## Rainy morning brings steady stream of voters to Center Street polls\n\n*02:24 PM ET, August 11 2026, April Quevedo*\n\nThe rain has not deterred a steady stream of voters from casting their ballot at the Milwaukee Public Library Center Street Branch, 2727 W. Fond Du Lac Ave. This year, the polling site serves two voting wards: 147 (Metcalfe Park) and 143 (Amani).\n\nThe library sits near the intersection of West Center Street, West Fond du Lac Avenue and North 27th Street – the intersection with the most motor vehicle collisions in the city. To help keep voters walking to the polls safe, the Milwaukee Police Department's Safety Division has placed crossing guards at all crosswalks leading to the library. The guards will be present until 8:30 p.m.\n\nLinsday Jines, Ward 147 polling captain, said there have been no issues with voting equipment. She expects the polls to get busier as residents being getting off work around 5 p.m.\n\nAmong morning voters was Shirley Pierce, an Amani resident and lifelong Milwaukeean. While she did not share who she cast her ballot for, Pierce said she is a lifelong Democrat who had issues like community development and resources for seniors at the top of her mind when deciding which candidate to support.\n\nMinnie Hicks, a Metcalfe Park resident, said housing affordability was the top issue behind her decision.\n\nShe stressed the importance of voting to bring about positive changes in Milwaukee and across the state.\n\n\"Today is a very important day,\" Hicks said. \"I want to be seen; I want to be heard; I want to see changes within Wisconsin.\"\n\n## Green Bay interim clerk: Polling place opened late, but primary election morning has gone smoothly\n\n*12:33 PM ET, August 11 2026, Jeff Bollier*\n\nCity of Green Bay Interim Clerk Stacy Menke said as of 10:30 a.m. the Aug. 11 primary election is off to a good start although one polling location wasn't able to open on time.\n\nVoters in Ward 44 had to wait until almost 7:30 a.m. to cast their ballot at Bridge Point Church, 2421 W. Point Road, because poll workers couldn't get into the building until after polls opened at 7 a.m., Menke said.\n\nOtherwise, she said voting and absentee ballot processing has gone smoothly this morning.\n\nCity of Green Bay Interim Clerk Stacy Menke discusses voter activity during the first hours of voting in the Aug. 11, 2026 primary election.\n\nMore than 1,000 ballots have been cast at polling locations today while the city's central count has processed 933 absentee ballots, about 20% of the 4,346 absentee ballots returned as of 5 p.m. Aug. 10.\n\nThe city issued a total of 6,040 absentee ballots and anyone who still needs to return now can only do so by dropping it off by 8 p.m. Tuesday at the City Clerk's office in City Hall, 100 N. Jefferson St.\n\n## Voting machines playing a role in this year's primary\n\n*12:28 PM ET, August 11 2026, Lance Schulteis*\n\nMilwaukee Firehouse Station 6 polling chief Emma Nelson said the election commission is encouraging voters to use express voting in this year's primary. She said the machines have been used in early voting for years as an accessibility device or for individuals without English as their first language.\n\nBut it also makes ballots easier to read and reduces the chance of errors, she said, which is helpful in partisan primary elections where voters only mark for one party. After voters tap through the options on the screen, their selections are recorded and counted the same way as a traditional paper ballot.\n\nRenee LaFleur, 61, cast her ballot with an express machine at Nelson's polling location, and she said that she felt the error risk was lower.\n\n\"I've made a mistake once before. My mom's made a mistake,\" LaFleur said. \"They have to change it, invalidate the ballot, then you get a new one and it's a whole thing. Here, you just back it up and fix it.\"\n\n## Wedgewood Park International School sees steady turnout, despite rain\n\n*12:07 PM ET, August 11 2026, Shane Colpoys*\n\nAs of 10 a.m, the rain has consistently started to fall outside of Wedgewood Park International School, where just over 60 voters have cast their ballots in the partisan primary, according to Polling chief Jim Godin.\n\nGodin said he's seen a steady flow of voters this morning and nothing \"out of the ordinary\" for this type of election.\n\nMostly older voters have walked in. Mark Winski, 67, said he is voting for \"freedom.\"\n\n\"The economy. I want to get out of Iran and that's about it,\" Winski said when asked about his top issues in this election. He said he's looking for a conservative candidate for governor.\n\nKerry and John Tanner, 75, said free and fair elections and education are on the top of their mind. They also acknowledged concerns over Francesca Hong's electability statewide.\n\n\"I know she's going to have a hard slug,\" Kerry Tanner said. \"But I can't stand back and say well, I don't think she's winnable and vote for the other guy.\"\n\n## Firehouse polling off to smooth start with voting machines\n\n*10:53 AM ET, August 11 2026, Lance Schulteis*\n\nJust prior to 9:30 a.m, 75 people had cast votes at Milwaukee Firehouse Station 6, 1693 N Franklin Pl. Polling chief Emma Nelson said there had not been any long waits or issues, calling turnout \"a steady trickle.\" She estimated nearly half of voters used express voting machines, which the election commission has been encouraging.\n\n\"It seems to be helpful in reducing errors and voters seem to be eager to give that a try,\" she said.Mary Kashian, 74, used the express machine to cast her vote. An educator for four decades, Kashian said she voted with young people on her mind.\n\n\"Young people can't afford to do the things that I did when I was that age,\" Kashian said. \"It always makes me a little said. I just feel like something's got to give here.\"\n\n## Starting the day off well in De Pere\n\n*09:35 AM ET, August 11 2026, Nadia Scharf*\n\nAbout 30 minutes after polls opened at the De Pere Community Center, 600 Grant St., 20 voters had cast their ballots, chief inspector Tracy Kamps said. It's gone well so far, he said.\n\nAt that time, Sue Albers was leaving the building after having just voted. When asked why she came out to vote, her answer was simple.\n\n\"It's our duty,\" Albers, 71, said. \"As an American.\"\n\n## Election Day starts at Celebration Church in Green Bay\n\n*09:27 AM ET, August 11 2026, Ariel Perez*\n\nThe polls at Celebration Church, 3475 Humboldt Road, in Green Bay opened at 7 a.m. and at 7:15, close to 30 people had already cast their votes.\n\nMatt Hulback, chief inspector for ward 8, said 1,600 people are registered to vote at this location.\n\n\"We usually see a lower turnout for the primaries, around 30%,\" Hulback said.\n\nHulback said around 500 people voted in the spring elections, April 7, which decided Supreme Court Justice Chris Taylor and two city council seats in districts 1 and 2.\n\n## What will voter turnout be in Wisconsin?\n\n*09:00 AM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nTypically, only 25% of the state's voting-age population has turned out in recent fall primaries, even in 2018, when multiple Democratic candidates were competing for the nomination to become governor.\n\nThere are signs that turnout could be higher, including a close margin in the U.S. Senate primary in Michigan, which many political observers have compared to the Democratic primary here.\n\n\"That can create an expectation that perhaps the Wisconsin race could be closer than people might imagine,\" University of Wisconsin-La Crosse political science professor Anthony Chergosky said. \"There has been a surge of national attention on Wisconsin as a potential proving ground for the electoral viability of democratic socialism.\"\n\nStill, early voting was lagging one week before Aug. 11 compared to the same time frame in Wisconsin's April election. Many Democratic absentee voters may have waited to complete their ballots due to a rapidly changing candidate list – but they should call their clerk about how to return them today instead of placing them in the mail.\n\nRead more about the early voting trends here.\n\n## What do the Wisconsin governor polls show?\n\n*08:30 AM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nIn the latest Marquette University Law School poll, among the highest-rated in the country, Francesca Hong had a sizable lead, at 38% of registered Democratic primary voters.\n\nBut the poll was conducted between July 22 and 27, before Mandela Barnes, who was polling in second place, dropped out of the race. From there, poll director Charles Franklin reallocated his supporters' second choices to other candidates.\n\nThat had the effect of boosting Hong's lead to over 40%, bringing Crowley into the mid-teens, and also raising the number of undecided voters to the low-40s, since many Barnes voters didn't immediately have a second choice.\n\n\"There could be a lot of uncertainty because of four in 10 primary voters saying they don't know who to vote for – in that poll,\" Franklin told the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel Aug. 5. \"More than ever, the message is that the polls don't vote, people do.\"\n\nOther polls have similarly found Hong leading by at least double digits, according to a New York Times poll tracker. Crowley's campaign has argued that closer-than-expected results in the Michigan U.S. Senate race could mean Hong's margin isn't as wide as polling suggests.\n\n\"Maybe the wilder fluctuations in the Wisconsin primary make it more possible for this kind of error to happen, but it would be substantial at more than 20 points,\" University of Wisconsin-Madison political science professor Barry Burden previously told the Journal Sentinel.\n\n## Who dropped out of the Wisconsin governor race? Can voters get a redo?\n\n*08:00 AM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nMandela Barnes, Sara Rodriguez and Missy Hughes dropped out of the race after the Wisconsin Elections Commission met June 9 to finalize the ballot. So, their names still appear as options, though they are no longer campaigning to become governor.\n\nIf you already sent back an Aug. 11 absentee ballot, you can't get a \"redo\" if you've changed your mind. Absentee voters who already voted for Barnes, Rodriguez or Hughes can't request a new ballot to vote for another candidate.\n\nThree Dane County voters, backed by the Democratic Party of Wisconsin, sued the WEC over its recent guidance that prohibits those redoes. A judge upheld the rule July 29, meaning voters who already submitted absentee ballots can't get a redo.\n\nHowever, if an absentee voter has not already mailed back or otherwise returned their ballot and mistakenly fills out their ballot for the wrong candidate, the clerk can issue a replacement, according to the WEC. The restriction kicks in once the voter sends back or returns their ballot.\n\n## What does Wisconsin vote for today?\n\n*07:30 AM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nToday's election is a partisan primary, meaning voters will choose which candidates they want to represent their party in the Nov. 3 general election.\n\nAt the top of the ballot is the governor's race, featuring four Democrats and two Republicans competing for the nomination. Three candidates – Mandela Barnes, Sara Rodriguez and Missy Hughes – dropped out but still appear on the ballot. The candidates still running are:\n\n* **Tom Tiffany**, Republican, congressman representing northern Wisconsin\n* **Andy Manske**, Republican, medical services technician\n* **Francesca Hong**, Democrat, state representative from Madison\n* **David Crowley**, Democrat, Milwaukee County executive and former state representative\n* **Joel Brennan**, Democrat, former Evers Cabinet secretary and president of Greater Milwaukee Committee\n* **Kelda Roys**, Democrat, state senator representing Madison\n\nOther races include state constitutional offices, state Legislature, U.S. House seats, sheriffs and other partisan local offices. See additional candidate guides below:\n\n* Lieutenant governor\n* Secretary of state\n* State treasurer\n* 1st Congressional District (southeastern Wisconsin)\n* 3rd Congressional District (western Wisconsin)\n* 4th Congressional District (much of Milwaukee County)\n* 6th Congressional District (east-central Wisconsin)\n* 7th Congressional District (northern Wisconsin)\n\nThere are no statewide referendum questions on the Aug. 11 ballot. Only four communities – Delafield, Rochester, Wrightstown and the Galesville-Ettrick-Trempealeau School District – have local fire/EMS, school funding or data center referendums.\n\nYou can see a preview of your ballot by entering your address at myvote.wi.gov.\n\n## What should I do if I still have an absentee ballot?\n\n*07:00 AM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nIf you still have a completed absentee ballot laying around, do not put it in the mail. Your local clerk must have your ballot by 8 p.m. today for your votes to count, and it won't get mailed in time. Absentee ballot postmarks essentially don't matter in Wisconsin.\n\nIt's best to call your city, town or village clerk for instructions on returning your absentee ballot, since some have different preferences. They may ask you to take your ballot to your assigned polling place or central count location.\n\nSome communities have absentee ballot drop boxes, but voters should pay attention to posted instructions. Milwaukee's 14 drop boxes close at 6 p.m. on Election Day, for example.\n\nIf you never filled out an absentee ballot you got in the mail, you can instead vote at your polling place Aug. 11, according to Wisconsin Elections Commission guidance. But if you already mailed back or hand-delivered your absentee ballot, you can't vote in-person on Election Day, even if your ballot hasn't arrived.\n\n## Can I register at the polls in Wisconsin?\n\n*06:30 AM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nYes. You can register at your polling place in Wisconsin, including updating your registration if you've changed your name or moved addresses since the last election you voted in.\n\nTo register, you must bring a proof of residence document that shows your current address. You can show a hard copy or electronic version on your phone. Proof of residence documents include:\n\n* **A driver's license or state ID card**, if it has your current address\n* **A bank or credit card statement**\n* **A paycheck or paystub**\n* **A recent utility bill**\n* **A property tax bill or receipt**\n* **A residential lease**, if it's valid on the date of registration.\n* **A university or college ID card with your photo.** This can only be used if you have a fee receipt within the last nine months or if your college provides a housing list to the local clerk.\n* **An intake document from a nursing home or assisted living facility**\n\nYou must live at the address you plan to vote from for at least 28 days by Election Day. If you've moved within Wisconsin less than 28 days before Aug. 11, you should vote at your previous polling place. Call your clerk with questions.\n\n## Do I need to bring a voter ID to the polls?\n\n*06:08 AM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nYes. You will be asked to show a valid photo ID at the polls. It does not have to show your current address. Acceptable photo IDs include:\n\n* **Wisconsin driver's license**\n* **State ID card** issued by the DMV\n* **Military or veteran's ID card**\n* **Tribal ID card**\n* **U.S. passport**\n* **Certificate of naturalization**\n* **College ID card**, if it includes the date it was issued, the student's signature and expires no later than two years after it was issued. Some universities issue separate ID cards that fit the requirements.\n\nYou may have heard about the SAVE Act, a bill pending in Congress that would require proof of citizenship to vote. It hasn't become law, so Wisconsin voters don't need to bring a separate document that indicates their citizenship status, such as a birth certificate.\n\nA federally compliant REAL ID, with the black star in the corner, is also not required to vote in Wisconsin.\n\nVoters who don't bring a valid photo ID will be offered a provisional ballot and must present an ID to their clerk within three days after the election for their votes to count.\n\n## When are polls open in Wisconsin today?\n\n*06:07 AM ET, August 11 2026, Hope Karnopp*\n\nPolls will be open from 7 a.m. to 8 p.m. statewide. As long as you're in line by 8 p.m., you will be allowed to vote.\n\nFind your polling place by typing in your address at myvote.wi.gov.\n\n*This article originally appeared on Milwaukee Journal Sentinel:* *Wisconsin election recap, results; Crowley wins in shocker over Hong*\n\n* About Our Ads\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAdvertisement","author":"Hope Karnopp, Ariel Perez, Nadia Scharf, Lance Schulteis, Shane Colpoys, Jeff Bollier, Jim Riccioli, Mary Spicuzza, Kathryn Byrnes, Erik S. Hanley, April Quevedo, Claudia Levens, Christopher Kuhagen, Alyssa N. Salcedo, Blaise Mesa, Jesse Lin, Adrienne Davis, Laura Schulte, Ricardo Torres, Duke Behnke, Vanessa Swales, Clara Strecker, Emmett Prosser, Peter Frank and Molly Beck, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel","published_date":"2026-08-11T10:00:53Z","latency_ms":1823.222431994509,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}